export const YOUTUBE_PACKAGING_REFERENCE = "=== Packaging rules: four-word title, face on camera, thumbnail rules ===\n# Packaging rules\n\nPackaging is how a video concept is translated into a specific title and thumbnail. The concept determines whether a video is worth making. The packaging determines whether anyone clicks on it. The best concept with bad packaging produces no views. Mediocre concepts with strong packaging outperform brilliant concepts with weak packaging every time.\n\n## Why this matters for Pulsify\n\nPulsify's clients spend time and resources producing content. Packaging failure wastes that production entirely — the video gets made, posted, and ignored. Packaging rules are the set of constraints that Pulsify applies before any video is titled or thumbnailed, so that production effort is spent on ideas that have a documented path to performance.\n\n## Core mechanics\n\n### The four-word title rule\n\nAyman Arab's most specific packaging constraint: the title should be compressible to four words or fewer that carry the full weight of the concept.\n\nThe test: if you cover the full title and show someone only four words, do those four words make them want to click?\n\n- \"How to find winning products for your Shopify dropshipping store in 2025\" → the four-word core is \"Find Winning Dropshipping Products\" → does that work? Yes.\n- \"My honest thoughts on whether YouTube is worth your time as a high-ticket coach\" → the four-word core is... unclear. The title is over-written. Compress it.\n\nThis rule applies differently to complete vs. repeat title formats:\n\n**Complete title:** States the full concept so clearly the viewer knows exactly what they will get. No ambiguity. High search optimization. Example: \"How I Booked 14 Calls From a 400-View YouTube Video.\"\n\n**Repeat title format (not recommended for Pulsify's core content):** Titles that are ambiguous or curiosity-based without completing the promise. These optimize for click-through rate but reduce watch time from viewers who feel misled. Ayman recommends against repeat titles for authority-building channels.\n\n### Face on camera\n\nBoth Ayman and Alexei agree: the creator must be on camera. Not a cartoon avatar. Not a generic stock photo. The face.\n\nFor channels competing in the trust recession environment, a face on camera is not optional — it is the primary signal that a real person with real credentials is behind the content. AI voiceovers, stock footage, and anonymous production signal the opposite.\n\n**Thumbnail face requirements:**\n- Clear, recognizable face (no small insets)\n- Eye contact with the lens — not looking at the whiteboard, not looking off-screen\n- Expression matched to the emotion of the topic (confident for results-based topics, curious for breakdown topics)\n\n### The content angle formula\n\nAlexei's content angle formula: take a proven outlier concept from the broader market + apply the micro niche angle + verify the angle is different from existing coverage.\n\nThe formula prevents two failure modes:\n1. **Creating content nobody is searching for** — unique content with no demand produces zero views regardless of quality\n2. **Creating content that blends in** — copying a proven concept without a new angle produces a worse version of something that already exists\n\nThe sweet spot: a proven concept (someone else already validated that viewers want this) + a new angle (a different creator would not have made this exact video) + micro niche framing (speaks to the specific avatar, not the general market).\n\n### The hook and first-30-seconds rule\n\nThe packaging does not stop at the title and thumbnail. The hook — the first 30 seconds of the video — is the final packaging layer. If the title and thumbnail earn a click, the hook decides whether the viewer stays.\n\nAlexei's rule: the first 30 seconds must confirm that the video delivers exactly what the title promised. Not setup. Not context. Confirmation.\n\nFailing hook structure: \"Hey everyone, welcome back. Today I want to talk about something I've been thinking about a lot...\"\nWorking hook structure: \"In this video, I'll show you the exact three-step framework I used to book 14 calls from a single 400-view YouTube video — and why a 400-view video can outperform a 2-million-view video if it is built for the right funnel layer.\"\n\nThe hook must do three things in the first 30 seconds:\n1. Confirm the specific promise from the title\n2. Signal that the creator has authority to deliver on that promise (briefly — not a bio)\n3. Create an open loop: why is it worth watching all the way through?\n\n### The open loop\n\nAn open loop is a piece of information that cannot be resolved until the viewer watches more. Alexei uses it explicitly as a retention device.\n\nExamples:\n- \"Most people who try this make one mistake that eliminates all the benefit. I'll show you the mistake at the end.\" (Forces watch to the end)\n- \"The third reason is the one nobody talks about.\" (Forces forward progress to find the third reason)\n- \"Before I show you the system, I need to show you why the obvious alternative doesn't work.\" (Frames everything that follows as necessary context)\n\nOpen loops must be resolved. An unresolved open loop damages trust — the viewer feels misled, which damages retention on future videos.\n\n### Editing rules by audience type\n\nAyman's specific editing guidance based on audience financial profile:\n\n**B2B / high-income audience:**\n- No jump cuts\n- No intense music\n- Clear speech at natural pace\n- High-value content over entertainment value\n- Minimal visual effects\n- The viewer's time is money — do not waste it with production flair\n\n**B2C / general public:**\n- Jump cuts acceptable\n- Background music adds energy\n- More visual variety tolerated\n- Entertainment value matters alongside information value\n\nMost Pulsify clients serve B2B-adjacent audiences (coaches, operators, creators). Default to the B2B editing standard. A high-ticket operator does not want to feel like they are watching a TikTok content farm.\n\n### Thumbnail rules\n\nDerived from both Ayman and Alexei's frameworks:\n\n1. **One clear focal point** — face, text, or a single graphic. Not three competing elements.\n2. **Title and thumbnail must be connected but not redundant** — the thumbnail adds a visual element the title does not say, or vice versa. If the thumbnail just restates the title as a text overlay, one of them is wasted.\n3. **Contrast matters** — the thumbnail must pop in a sea of other thumbnails at small size. Test at 120px width.\n4. **Curiosity or proof** — thumbnails should create either curiosity (\"what is that result?\") or demonstrate proof (\"that number is real and I want to know how\"). Generic images accomplish neither.\n5. **A number is fine on its own. It turns toxic when you couple it with status.** Founder ruling, 2026-07-21, replacing the earlier blanket \"no income screenshots as thumbnails.\"\n\n   A real interface showing a real figure, on its own, reads as blunt transparency and it is catchy. A payout ledger with account IDs and dates, a revenue dashboard, a platform P&L: these are evidence, and evidence is what the trust recession actually rewards. The viewer can go and check.\n\n   The same number becomes obnoxious the moment it is coupled with watches, cars, or lifestyle. At that point the number stops being evidence and becomes a status claim, which is the signal that pre-categorises the creator as another scam. **The variable is the coupling, never the figure.**\n\n   Still banned: a figure with no interface behind it, a number the creator cannot show inside the video, any figure paired with a lifestyle signal, and hype framing around it (exclamation marks, \"PRINTED\", shock faces). Consistent with [[concepts/trust-recession]], which already scoped this correctly as \"income screenshots without context or audit trail.\"\n\n6. **Layout comes from [[content-engine/thumbnail-layout-patterns]]** — four patterns (raw photo, board breakdown, screen recording, two people in a real location) with the text treatment scoped per pattern. Added 2026-07-21.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n=== Thumbnail layout patterns A to D and the text treatment rule (canonical where older guidance disagrees) ===\n# Thumbnail layout patterns\n\nThe four layouts Pulsify actually builds, extracted from the 20 reference thumbnails the founder supplied on 2026-07-21. Every one of them is a real photo or a real screen. None are AI-generated scenes. This page is the layout half of packaging; [[content-engine/packaging-rules]] governs the title and thumbnail pairing, and [[content-engine/thumbnail-photo-cleanup-prompts]] holds the retouch prompts.\n\n## Why this matters for Pulsify\n\nThe founder's standing rule on 2026-07-21: on a thumbnail we want to look as normal as humanly possible, because the click is only worth having if the viewer arrives thinking this person is not a guru. The reference set is how that gets executed without the thumbnail becoming invisible. Attribution note: the founder named Jonas, Mason, Dustin and Nick as sources across the set but did not map names to files, so this page describes patterns and does not assign a creator to any specific image.\n\n## The retouch standard (applies to every pattern)\n\nFour of the references are the same founder photo at four degrees of retouch. What stayed constant is the standard:\n\n- The person is untouched. Face, expression, hair, beard, skin texture, pose, hands, clothing all exactly as photographed. No beautifying, reshaping, smoothing, or slimming.\n- The real room stays the real room. Radiator, curtain, bed, mic arm all still present. Tidied, never swapped for a studio.\n- No zoom, no reframing of the subject. The figure just reads better.\n- Contrast and clarity lifted slightly so the subject separates from the background. Lighting stays natural.\n- Zero text on any of the four.\n\nThe move is not \"make it prettier.\" It is \"remove what competes with the subject.\" Same rule already written in [[content-engine/thumbnail-photo-cleanup-prompts]] prompt 1.\n\n## Pattern A — Raw photo, no text\n\nThe founder's four retouches. One person, real room, direct eye contact, deadpan. A single prop carries the tension (in the reference set, a $100 bill held to camera). No headline, no callouts, no arrows.\n\nUse when the image alone creates curiosity and the title carries the whole promise. This is the most \"normal human\" end of the range and the least guru-coded.\n\n## Pattern B — Board breakdown\n\nThe board fills the frame and is the background. A person is either physically at a real whiteboard, or present as a small inset. Short headline, often black uppercase on a yellow bar. One to three small labels with hand-drawn arrows pointing at specific parts of the board. App icons used as shorthand so words are not wasted.\n\nThe board must be a real board with real content on it. In the references the boards are legible systems, not decorative shapes.\n\nUse for system and framework explainers. This is the [[content-engine/ai-content-workflow-thumbnail-profile]] Miro Proof Board direction generalised.\n\n## Pattern C — Screen recording\n\n**One number, enormous, plus a large face.** This is the part that is easy to get wrong, so it is stated first. In every working reference the key figure is one of the largest objects in the frame, often a third of its width, and the face is big and well lit. None of them show a table, a list, or a full dashboard at its native scale.\n\n> ⚠️ Corrected 2026-07-21. This section previously said \"the real interface fills the frame at real scale\" with the face in a small corner inset. That reading produced two failed thumbnails: a payout ledger showing twelve rows at native scale and a chart with a small dark face, both of which turned to mush at 120px. Cropping a dense interface only yields a smaller dense interface. The interface is the evidence; one number from it is the subject.\n\nThe face sits in a corner as a webcam-style overlay, but large enough to read as a person rather than a stamp. Sometimes a red circle or arrow around the number. Text ranges from a short headline down to none at all, since the number is already carrying the message.\n\nThe discipline that separates the good ones from a raw screenshot: browser chrome, sidebars, taskbars and desktop clutter are cropped out or covered. Only the interface that matters survives.\n\nUse for anything where the proof is on a screen. This is the pattern for trade recaps, trade breakdowns, dashboards, and payout ledgers.\n\n**On showing the number (founder ruling, 2026-07-21).** A real figure on a real interface is blunt transparency and it is catchy, so show it. The ledger, the P&L, the payout column with its account IDs and dates: that is the asset, not a liability. What makes a number obnoxious is coupling it with watches, cars or lifestyle, at which point it stops being evidence and becomes a status claim. The variable is the coupling, never the figure. Full rule in [[content-engine/packaging-rules]] rule 5.\n\n## Pattern D — Two people, real location\n\nTwo people mid-action in a real place: typing at a desk, talking at a table, pointing at a board. Real office, real city window, real interior. Never an AI background, and the founder specifically noted that the original real background is what makes these work.\n\nText is restrained here. In the references it is either a yellow-bar headline with small labelled arrows, or plain speech bubbles carrying the question the video answers, or a single elegant two-word title with one arrow. Numbers are often absent entirely.\n\nUse for interviews, mentor-and-student, and any video where the second person is part of the proof.\n\n## Text treatment\n\nThe set spans a wide range and the range itself is the lesson.\n\nRestrained end: no text at all, or a plain question in a speech bubble, or a two-word title. Loud end: yellow bar, black uppercase, a large revenue figure, an exclamation mark.\n\nThe founder's 2026-07-21 direction pulls toward the restrained end. Hype adjectives, exclamation marks, shock faces and stacked numbers read as guru and cost the trust the click was supposed to buy.\n\n### The rule (founder-ratified 2026-07-21)\n\n**The yellow bar survives on Pattern B only.** On a board breakdown the headline is structural: the board is unreadable at 120px, so a short bold line is the only thing telling the viewer what they are looking at. Black uppercase on a yellow bar stays the treatment there.\n\n**Pattern A and Pattern C go quiet.** A raw photo carries its own tension and a real interface carries its own number, so neither needs a shouted headline. Options in order of preference: no text at all, a plain short label, or a question in a speech bubble. Lowercase or sentence case. No yellow bar, no all-caps headline, no exclamation marks, no stacked figures.\n\n**Pattern D goes quiet too.** The references that work carry a speech-bubble question or two plain words, and mostly no numbers.\n\nBanned across every pattern: hype adjectives, exclamation marks, shock faces, and any number that is not already visible on the real screen behind it.\n\n> ⚠️ Contradiction resolved: this supersedes [[content-engine/thumbnail-photo-cleanup-prompts]] prompt 2, which specified the yellow bar generically, and narrows [[content-engine/ai-content-workflow-thumbnail-profile]]'s \"do not soften it\" to Pattern B only. Both pages updated 2026-07-21. The titles-thumbnails skill still makes thumbnail text mandatory at four words max and has not been updated; treat this page as canonical where they disagree, per the repo-is-canonical rule.\n\n\n\n\n## The 120px test (run it before shipping any thumbnail)\n\nDownscale the candidate to 120px wide and look at it. If the number is not readable and the face is not recognisable at that size, it fails, regardless of how good it looks at full resolution. This caught two thumbnails that looked acceptable at 1280px and were unusable at real feed size.\n\n```python\nfrom PIL import Image\nImage.open('candidate.jpg').resize((120, 68)).resize((600, 340)).save('test.png')\n```";
